✅ Fractional Knapsack Problem – Theory (Greedy Method)

You are given:

A knapsack with capacity W

n items, each with:

weight wi

profit/value pi

In the fractional knapsack, you are allowed to take fractions of an item
(e.g., you can take half of an item).

✅ Goal:

Maximize total profit while ensuring total weight ≤ W.

✅ Key Idea of the Greedy Method

The greedy method works because in the fractional knapsack, the best strategy is to:

✅ Take items in decreasing order of value/weight ratio (pi/wi)

This ratio tells you how much profit you get per unit weight.

Steps:

Calculate profit-to-weight ratio for each item.

Sort the items in decreasing order of this ratio.

Start picking items:

If the whole item fits → pick it.

If not → take a fraction of the item equal to remaining knapsack capacity.

Stop when the knapsack is full.

This strategy guarantees the optimal solution, unlike 0/1 knapsack (which needs DP).

✅ Why greedy works? (Conceptual Reasoning)

Because profits add linearly with fractions.

If item A gives more profit per unit weight than item B,
taking even a small amount of A is always better than B.

There is no dependency between items, so local optimal choice → global optimum.

✅ Time Complexity

Breaking down the operations:

Computing ratios → O(n)

Sorting items by ratio → O(n log n) (dominant term)

Picking items → O(n)

✅ Overall Time Complexity: O(n log n)
(due to sorting)

✅ Space Complexity

Need an array of items with (profit, weight, ratio)

Sorting usually done in-place

✅ Space Complexity: O(1) to O(n)
(depending on sorting method; typically O(1) extra)

✅ Step Count (High Level)

Compute all ratios → n steps

Sort → approx n log n comparisons

Pick items → up to n iterations

So overall steps ≈ n log n

