Fractional Knapsack (Greedy) – Theory

In the fractional knapsack problem, we can take any fraction of an item. To maximize total profit, we compute the profit/weight ratio for each item and sort them in decreasing order. We then take items according to this order. If the remaining capacity can accommodate the whole item, we take it; otherwise, we take a fraction of that item equal to the available capacity. This greedy approach always gives the optimal solution because each fraction contributes profit linearly based on its ratio.

Time Complexity: O(n log n) (due to sorting)

Space Complexity: O(1) to O(n) depending on implementation

Greedy Choice: Highest profit-to-weight ratio first

Step Count: ~n log n
